          "GI Bill and military-benefit callers need a team that knows benefits. If you don't have that team, fail military-benefit-only callers or route them to the people who do. Transferring a benefits-driven caller into a civilian advisor who has to read from a FAQ is a long, billable, unenrollable conversation. Duration will look great. Starts will not.",
          "WIOA, voc rehab, and employer-contract seats are real and slow. They can be excellent enrollments. They are often bad live-transfer inventory because the paperwork isn't a one-call close. If those funding paths are core to a program, consider CPL plus appointment, not a high concurrency transfer dump. If they're rare exceptions, don't let the IVR pretend everyone is a WIOA candidate.",
          "Incentivized offers (gift cards, laptops, 'paid to start school') attract a caller who is shopping the incentive, not the credential. Some of that can still start. A lot of it withdraws or never completes. If your brand will not allow incentive creative, say so. We will not run it. If your brand does allow it, expect a different quality mix and don't compare it to a clean search path on the same CPA.",
          "Credit-history and defaulted-federal-aid issues kill starts late. You cannot fully screen that in an IVR without becoming a financial-aid office. You can ask 'have you used federal student aid before' if your school has a hard stop on default without rehab. Use hard stops only when they are real. Every extra fail lowers volume. That's acceptable when the fail would have been a 20-minute advisor dead end.",
